UnixLib tests (riscos-unixlib)
==============================

Programs built with the new UnixLib, to check /dev/dsp, /dev/midi and
two fixes from the Warzone 2100 port.
Double-click the Obey files. Each first prints the release it came from
(for example "UnixLibTests 5.0.3.1-rc8: ForkOnly"). The programs are Absolute (AIF) files, so the
ELF loader is not needed. The modules for SharedSoundBuffer output
(SharedSound, StreamManager, SharedSoundBuffer) must be loaded.
SharedSound is part of RISC OS; SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager come
from ssb.zip: download it from Andrew Sellors' RDPClient page,
  https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html
and merge its !System into yours. John Duffell's own site (now on the
Internet Archive) has more details:
  https://web.archive.org/web/20110920080106/http://www.duffell.riscos.me.uk/
Then see !MIDISynth's LoadSound or OpenTTD's !Run, or run:
  RMEnsure SharedSound 1.07 RMLoad System:Modules.SSound
  RMEnsure StreamManager 0.03 RMLoad System:Modules.StreamMan
  RMEnsure SharedSoundBuffer 0.07 RMLoad System:Modules.SSBuffer

Tone        5 s chord. First line says which output is used.
ToneMono8   8-bit mono 11 kHz        ) formats UnixLib converts for
ToneULaw    mu-law mono 8 kHz        ) SharedSoundBuffer: each should
ToneBE      16-bit big-endian 22 kHz ) sound like the same chord
ToneNB      non-blocking writes, small fragments
ToneDR      the old DigitalRenderer path
Mix         two programs play at once (should hear both)
ExitBug     DigitalRenderer plays; another program quits after 3 s:
            the sound must carry on (the long-standing bug)
ExitBugSSB  the same with SharedSoundBuffer
MIDI        scale and chords through /dev/midi. Needs the MIDISynth
            *module* (not written yet; the !MIDISynth app is not a
            module) or the MIDI module with a MIDI device. Without
            either it says "Operation not supported by device": that's
            the expected result for now.

Clock       (5.0.3.11, T6) the monotonic clock: 200000 reads never go
            back, finer than a centisecond, keeps pace with
            OS_ReadMonotonicTime over 3 s, nanosleep 20 ms. Each line must
            say ok, then PASSED; please say what a read costs and which
            way it reads (Linux's clock or the HAL counter).
EABIExit    does ARMEABISupport still know the program when it exits?
            A Wimp task with two threads polls for 3 s, then
            Wimp_CloseDown and exit (as Reel quits). It logs, at each
            stage, the physical page at &8000 (how ARMEABISupport tells
            programs apart) and whether ARMEABISupport finds the program,
            to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.EABIExit, and shows that file: PASS or
            FAIL. A FAIL means its record (and stacks) stay behind, which
            can make a later program trap at start-up ("code 6").
            EABIExitNoClose  without Wimp_CloseDown
            EABIExitBig      with Reel's 40 MB WimpSlot
            EABIExitPlain    a plain command-line program
            EABIExitRunning  exits with its threads still running
            After each, *ARMEABISupport_Info should list no App left by it.

Once        pthread_once: an init routine that waits for another thread
            in pthread_once (stopped with "Deadlocked" before 5.0.3.11, P3),
            three callers of one slow init routine, a cancelled init
            routine. Must print PASS
PageMove    a reproducer: claims the physical page at the program's own
            &8000, which RISC OS then replaces, and asks whether
            ARMEABISupport still finds the program. With ARMEABISupport
            1.08 it prints REPRODUCED and leaves an App in
            *ARMEABISupport_Info (reboot afterwards). Reproduced on a
            Pi 4 (RISC OS 5, ARMEABISupport 1.08), 2026-10-03.

ExitJoin    a thread joined from an atexit() handler: must print PASS,
            no "Aborted" (Warzone 2100 aborted like this on quit)
FsyncRO     fsync() on a read-only file: must print PASS
TimedWait   (5.0.3.11, P4) pthread_cond_timedwait on CLOCK_REALTIME and
            CLOCK_MONOTONIC (a 300 ms wait must take 300 ms), passed
            deadlines, a signal before the deadline; sem_wait,
            sem_timedwait and 20000 posts between two threads; a thread
            cancelled in sem_wait. Must print PASS.
Ticker      two busy threads in a Wimp task for 20 s while other tasks run
            (have Alarm, a clock or Organizer running). Ends with an error
            box saying PASS: both threads counted more than 0 although the
            task polls far more often than every 2 cs (5.0.3.1; before,
            they got no time and were counted 0). No other program may
            crash. The log
            <Wimp$ScrapDir>.tickertest also shows whether the task handle
            UnixLib saw at start-up matches Wimp_Initialise's.
TickerEarly the same with the threads started before Wimp_Initialise: the
            ticker then stays on while other tasks run (worst case)
TickerStartTask
            as Ticker, and every 5 s it starts a child task (TickerChild,
            which busy-waits 1 s) with Wimp_StartTask
            Run the three Ticker tests twice: first as they are (the
            lines end via=RMA), then after LoadTicker (the PThreadTicker
            module from this folder, 0.03: via=module; an older
            PThreadTicker isn't used, so the lines stay via=RMA). The
            post_switches count is how often the threads were switched
            as Wimp_Poll returned. To keep the module,
            copy PThrTicker into !Boot.Resources.!System.310.Modules
            and RMEnsure it from a !Run file; it can't be killed while a
            program uses it.
            The three Ticker tests set UnixLib$TickerStats, so UnixLib
            appends a line of ticker counters to
            <Wimp$ScrapDir>.TickerStats as each program exits (the child
            adds its own lines). Please send that file and
            <Wimp$ScrapDir>.tickertest after each run (or after all three:
            TickerStats is appended to, tickertest is overwritten).
Sched       sched_get_priority_min/max and pthread_setschedparam:
            must print PASS
ToneStdio   3 s of tone written to /dev/dsp with fwrite while a thread
            runs: must print PASS (stopped with "pthread_yield called with
            context switching disabled" before 5.0.3.3-rc1).
            ToneStdioDR: the same through DigitalRenderer
EventFD     eventfd between threads: two threads write for about 10
            seconds while the main thread reads; every write must be
            counted once. Then a blocking read woken by another thread,
            and (5.0.3.3-rc1) a blocked read that must be cancellable.
            Must print PASS (needs 5.0.3.2-rc2, and 5.0.3.3-rc1 for the
            cancel part). The first two parts PASS on a Pi 4 with
            5.0.3.2-rc2, 2026-10-03
LargeFile   files over 2GB (built with -D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64): makes a
            3GB file LargeTest in this folder, checks seeking, sizes and
            data past 2GB, deletes it; must print PASS. Needs 3GB free on
            a filing system with files over 2GB, and a few minutes
BigHeap     a heap past 128 MB: allocates up to 320 MB in 16 MB blocks,
            checks them, lists the heap's dynamic areas ("UnixLibTest
            Heap", "UnixLibTest Heap 2"...), then frees them and allocates
            one 200 MB block (bigger than one area). Must print PASS.
            Needs about 350 MB free.
HeapCheck   run after BigHeap has quit: must print PASS (no "UnixLibTest
            Heap" area left behind; the Task Manager should agree)
ForkExec    vfork + an exec that fails, and system(), from a program
            with two busy threads, three times each: the threads must keep
            running. With the PThreadTicker module loaded (LoadTicker), it
            also checks the module still counts the program, and that
            *RMKill PThreadTicker is refused while it runs. Must print
            PASS. Run it once before LoadTicker and once after.
ForkOnly    fork() three times, no threads: must print PASS.
ForkThreads fork() from a program with two busy threads (as ForkExec, with
            fork added): must print PASS. With 5.0.3.1-rc4 this aborted
            ("abort on data transfer") as fork returned in the parent.
FiniArray   a C++ program: at exit the atexit() function, the static
            destructor and two __attribute__((destructor)) functions must
            run in that order (prints ASFL and PASS; 5.0.3.11, I1)
ForkWorker  fork() with a worker thread running: the child must not run
            the parent's thread (its join gives ESRCH), the parent's
            worker must carry on unharmed, and a change an atfork prepare
            handler makes to main's frame must be kept. Must print PASS
            (needs 5.0.3.3-rc1 or later)
HeapProbe   a probe, not a pass/fail test: can one heap block be bigger
            than 128 MB? Tries (A) a second dynamic area placed right
            after the first, and (B) "physical memory pool" areas of
            256 MB, 512 MB and 1 GB with 140 MB mapped in. Removes every
            area it makes. Please send DAProbeLog (written next to it).
            Needs about 300 MB free.
Preempt     two threads that never yield, and a main thread that never
            yields either: only the thread ticker switches them. Both must
            count past 200000; must print PASS.
ITimer      interval timers: a 100 ms interval must give 6 alarms in under
            2 s (before 5.0.3.4 only the first came), and a 1 s interval 3
            alarms about 1 s apart. Must print PASS.
Fault       a SIGILL caught by a handler ("caught SIGILL: ok"), then an
            uncaught one: must print "Fatal signal received: Illegal
            instruction" and a backtrace, and end the program. Nothing else
            on the machine should be affected.
KeyDest     (5.0.3.11, K13) for about 10 s, threads that set 8
            thread-specific values each end while 4 other threads malloc
            and free without stopping: every destructor runs once and the
            heap stays intact. Must print PASS.
LSeek       (5.0.3.9) lseek past the end of a file: a read there gives
            end of file and the file stays empty, a write fills the gap
            with zeros, SEEK_CUR and SEEK_END count from the right place,
            and stdio's fseek then fputc. Each line must say ok, then
            PASS. It uses <Wimp$ScrapDir>; to try another filing system,
            give a directory in Unix form, e.g. "LSeek /RAM::RamDisc0.$".
RwLock      (5.0.3.11) pthread_rwlock_init over memory that looks like a
            lock already (EBUSY before, and GLib aborted); the new lock
            then takes two readers, then a writer. Must print PASS.
Rename      rename() of files whose type isn't the one MimeMap gives
            their extension (a Text "rntest.xml" renamed to .xml.bak, as
            Claws Mail does): each line must say ok, then PASS. Run it in
            the desktop: without MimeMap it says it proves nothing.
SockPair    (5.0.3.11) socketpair over loopback TCP (it never worked
            before) and mkdtemp: messages both ways, 200 one-byte round
            trips with the time taken, AF_UNIX, 50 pairs, a temporary
            directory. Needs the Internet module. Each line must say ok,
            then PASS; please say how long the round trips took.
Procs       (5.0.3.11, C7) sysconf's processor count: 1, or what the
            variable UnixLib$Processors says (3, 64, and 2 set as a
            number; 0, 65, "abc" and "3abc" are ignored). Must print PASS
            eight times.
Sem         semaphores: sem_timedwait times out after about 200 ms while
            another thread runs, wakes when posted, and rejects a bad
            time; a thread waiting in sem_wait takes no processor time;
            100000 waits and posts lose no memory; and a sem_post from a
            SIGALRM handler, with every thread waiting, ends the wait.
            Each line must say ok, then PASS.
Shm         shared memory (shm_open, ftruncate, mmap twice, msync, dup,
            close, shm_unlink), a file opened with O_SHAREDFILE, and
            (5.0.3.8) a PROT_NONE anonymous mapping and a PROT_READ
            mapping of a file, which must not abort. The
            first line says which ARMEABISupport it found (1.05, or 1.07
            or later); it must work with either. Each line must say ok,
            then PASS. Please say which version it reported.
FirstTouch  (5.0.3.11, M3, B2) memcpy, memmove, memset, mempcpy and bcopy
            into and out of stack pages never touched before (in a
            thread with a 4 MB stack) and fresh mmap pages; setjmp into
            a fresh page; and bcopy copies the right way. Before rc8 it
            could die (an abort, or "Unixlib detected that ARMEABISupport
            could not handle an abort"). Must print PASS.
Search      (5.0.3.11, C8) strstr, strrchr, strspn, strcspn, strpbrk
            and memmem give exactly the old results for every alignment
            and many near matches, then old and new are timed on a line
            of text. Must print PASS; please say the times.
Strings     (5.0.3.11, C5) strcmp, strncmp, memcmp, strchr, memchr,
            strcasecmp and strncasecmp give exactly the old results for
            every alignment, length and difference, then old and new are
            timed on 64-byte strings. Must print PASS; please say the
            times.
Suspend     (5.0.3.11) pthread_suspend_thread really stops a thread: a
            spinning thread's counter stays still while suspended, its
            registers can be read, it runs again when resumed, and a
            suspended waiter doesn't run when signalled until resumed.
            Must print PASS.
VFPPoll     floating point straight after Wimp_Poll (a probe for the
            Web Browser's "Illegal Instruction"): run from the desktop.
            It starts 50 short-lived UnixLib tasks (VFPChild) and polls,
            and after every Wimp_Poll and Wimp_StartTask it counts
            whether its own VFP context is active, another one, or none,
            then does some floating point. An error box gives the counts:
            PASS (always its own), DIFFERENT (finished, but another or no
            context was active at some point) or FAIL (Illegal
            instruction). VFPPollT is the same as Thumb code; VFPPollThr
            and VFPPollTThr add a second thread. Please run all four and
            send <Wimp$ScrapDir>.VFPPoll. On a Pi 4 (5.31) the ARM ones
            PASS and the Thumb ones say DIFFERENT: VFPSupport restarts
            Thumb code 2 bytes early after a lazy switch, which repeated
            a store (the "another" count is that store's value).
VFPPollTR   the Thumb build calling __pthread_vfp_reclaim after each
            return (5.0.3.8): must PASS. VFPPollTRThr with a second thread.

Please report: what each one printed, whether it sounded right, any
clicks or gaps, and the "max queued" figure.
